```

The `STRING_SWITCH_L` macro reads 4 bytes from `p`. If `p` points to a header name near the end of the buffer, this could read past the buffer.

But wait — the `header_start` array stores pointers into the buffer. The issue is that `header_start` is a fixed-size array of 64 pointers, but the code at lines 537-538 writes TWO entries per iteration. Let me re-examine:

```c
for (p = buffer + 1; n_headers < N_HEADER_START && buffer_end > p;) {
    ...
    header_start[n_headers++] = next_chr;
    header_start[n_headers++] = next_hdr;
    ...
}
```

When `n_headers = 62`: `62 < 64` is true, writes to `header_start[62]` (n_headers becomes 63), writes to `header_start[63]` (n_headers becomes 64). Then `64 < 64` is false, loop exits. This is fine — indices 0-63 are valid.

But what about the `goto process` path? If `next_hdr` is found and `buffer_end - next_chr != 2`, it writes to `header_start[n_headers++]` and then continues. The `goto process` only happens when `!next_hdr` or `buffer_end - next_chr == 2`.

Wait, I need to look at this more carefully. The `goto process` happens BEFORE writing to header_start. So the overflow would have to happen in the loop body itself.

